      "left": "'Can the arm reach around the pillar without hitting it?'",
      "right": "Can a path between two points avoid certain forbidden regions?"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Nothing physical has changed — everything about the problems has. And because every robot has a configuration, one set of tools serves grippers, wheels, snake spines and humanoids alike."
};

const cfgCountCloze = {
  "text": "A free rigid body in the plane needs %%0%% numbers — two positions, one rotation; in space it needs %%1%%. A revolute joint contributes %%2%% freedom; a ball-and-socket joint contributes %%3%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "3",
      "three"
    ],
    [
      "6",
      "six"
    ],
    [
      "1",
      "one"
    ],
    [
      "3",
      "three"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Plane, space, hinge, ball-and-socket."
};

const cfgGrublerSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this design audit is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Grübler's formula is honest bookkeeping for linkages whose joints form loops that constrain each other.",
    "The four-bar sits inside your windshield wiper and a thousand engines.",
    "With fewer degrees of freedom than actuators, a mechanism flops uncontrolled; with more, it binds and fights itself.",
    "A correct count is the design's certificate: the linkage moves predictably, never flops and never jams.",
    "One input — spin the crank — determines the whole four-bar's motion."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "It's the reverse: fewer degrees of freedom than actuators binds and fights itself; more, and it flops uncontrolled.",
  "why": "Get the count wrong in either direction and no quality of motor rescues you — which is why the formula delivers its verdict from a parts list, before anything is built."
};

const cfgJointsSort = {
  "prompt": "Why 6 and why 7 — file each reason:",
  "buckets": [
    "6 joints — the minimum",
    "7 joints — the redundancy"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "a free body in space needs 6 DOF — place the gripper anywhere, any orientation",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "the standard industrial arm",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "slack to reach around obstacles while holding the gripper pose fixed",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "elephant trunks and human arms — redundancy of the same kind",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "a 6-dimensional task surface carved inside a 7-dimensional space",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "self-motion: infinitely many points projecting onto each point of the surface",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "That infinity of configurations per pose is redundancy, and it is a feature — not a joint wasted."
};

const cfgSpacesCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two of the three spaces, side by side — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "Workspace",
    "text": "The set of points the end-effector can physically reach — a property of the arm's geometry, at most six dimensions, usually fewer."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "Task space",
    "text": "Whatever the job actually lives in — two dimensions for drawing on paper, six for spray-painting a car body, five or fewer for most assembly once fixtures handle alignment."
  },
  "why": "The third is configuration space — the joint values themselves. Planning happens in the biggest one: a humble tabletop wipe may demand navigating a 6-dimensional C-space while the hand traces a flat 2-dimensional path. The task never sees the difficulty; the configuration space carries it."
};

const cfgAnnulusPlace = {
  "prompt": "A two-link planar arm, links of one metre each. Where does the reachable set's outer radius sit?",
  "labels": [
    "0 m",
    "0.75 m",
    "1.5 m",
    "2.25 m",
    "3 m"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 3
  },
  "answer": 2,
  "tolerance": 0.2,
  "why": "Fully extended: 1 + 1 = 2 m. And the inner radius is 0 — the arm can fold the gripper all the way back to the shoulder — so the reachable set is not a disk but an annulus from 0 to 2 m. Most reachable points correspond to two configurations (elbow up, elbow down), which is why inverse kinematics is not one-to-one."
};

const cfgObstacleOrder = {
  "prompt": "From geometry nightmare to GPS-style map — order the move:",
  "items": [
    "The naive check: does a shaped, articulated robot intersect a shaped world, joint by joint — redone for every instant of every candidate path",
    "Compute, once, the set of configurations in which the robot would collide with anything",
    "Mark that whole set as forbidden terrain — the sofa becomes a curved region in joint-angle space",
    "The question 'can the arm reach the shelf?' becomes 'can a path dodge that region?'",
    "Exact computation is hard in high dimensions — so planners sample and test points instead of mapping the terrain in full"
  ],
  "why": "The same question a GPS answers, in stranger coordinates — and sampling is one more reason, not a compromise anyone celebrates."
};

const cfgGridEst = {
  "prompt": "Discretise every joint at a modest 63 values (0.1-radian steps). How many cells does the grid of a 2-DOF space have?",
  "unit": "cells",
  "answer": 3969,
  "factor": 1.3,
  "why": "63 × 63 = 3,969 — trivial. Six DOF is about 6.25 × 10¹⁰, dead on arrival; a humanoid's 30-plus DOF is physically absurd to store. Hence the sampling-based planners — RRT and probabilistic roadmaps — which never enumerate, trading guaranteed optimality for probabilistic completeness.",
  "context": "Grids die of dimensionality; samplers probe instead."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/robotics/foundational/configuration-space">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Robotics and it assumes no prior robotics at all:
    "configuration", "degree of freedom" and C-space are all defined here, and the only
    mathematics this lesson demands is multiply-and-subtract — the power of the idea is that
    it replaces tangled geometry with one clean abstraction.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the C-space move: a robot's whole configuration treated as a single point in an abstract space;</li>
    <li>count degrees of freedom with Grübler's formula for planar linkages — four-bar and slider–crank included;</li>
    <li>distinguish configuration space, workspace and task space, and say which one planning actually lives in;</li>
    <li>classify holonomic versus nonholonomic constraints, using the car and parallel parking as the test case;</li>
    <li>explain why world obstacles grow when mapped into C-space, and why sampling-based planners exist at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Robotics' most powerful single abstraction is also its least physical: replace the whole
    robot with one point. A robot arm's "position" is not one number but a list — every joint
    angle at once. The <strong>configuration space</strong> (C-space) takes that list and
    treats it as the coordinates of a single imaginary point: a six-jointed arm becomes one
    point drifting through a six-dimensional space, where each dimension is one joint's
    angle. Nothing about the robot has changed; everything about the <em>problems</em> has.
    "Where is the robot?" is now "where is the point?", and the fiendishly tangled question
    "can the arm reach around the pillar without hitting it?" becomes a clean geometric one:
    can a path be drawn between two points that avoids certain forbidden regions?
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each question with its C-space rewrite" widget="match" config={cfgMoveMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each question with its rewrite and watch the abstraction work: where is the robot
    becomes where is the point, a six-jointed arm becomes one point drifting through a six-
    dimensional space, and reaching around the pillar becomes path-finding between two
    points. Notice the why: nothing physical has changed — everything about the problems
    has.
  </p>
  <p>
    The value of the move is uniformity. Robots come in absurd variety — grippers, wheels,
    snake spines, humanoids — but every one of them has a configuration, and every
    configuration is a point in some space. Once the whole field agrees to phrase its
    problems inside C-space, one set of tools serves them all: the same mathematics that
    plans a two-joint gripper plans a thirty-joint walking machine, just in a bigger space.
    The dimension gets punishing, and much of the field's algorithmic ingenuity exists to
    survive it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This chapter covers the three ideas the abstraction stands on: how to count a robot's
    degrees of freedom honestly (Grübler's formula), how the task and the workspace relate
    to the space of configurations, and how constraints — especially the sneaky
    nonholonomic kind that make cars unable to slide sideways — shape what paths through
    that space are even possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the C-space move replaces the whole robot with one point — where is the robot becomes where
    is the point — and one set of tools then serves grippers, wheels, snake spines and
    humanoids alike.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from robot to point: the C-space move" widget="equationStep" config={dofCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are this chapter's whole argument in order:
    a robot state becomes a point; the dimension of that space is counted honestly with
    Grübler's formula; the room's obstacles reappear as forbidden regions of the space;
    planning collapses into path-finding between two points; and dimension — the space grows
    with every joint — is the enemy that kills grids and calls sampling-based planners into
    being. Each section below takes one step.
  </p>

  <h2>Degrees of freedom and Grübler's formula</h2>
  <p>
    The number of <em>degrees of freedom</em> (DOF) is the number of independent numbers
    needed to pin down the robot's configuration — the dimension of its C-space. A free
    rigid body in the plane needs three (two positions, one rotation); in space it needs
    six. Joints spend those freedoms: a revolute joint (a hinge) contributes one, a ball-
    and-socket joint three. So a robot arm with three hinged joints in the plane has
    DOF = 3 — three numbers describe it completely.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="fill the counting rules" widget="cloze" config={cfgCountCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Fill the counting rules from the hint: three numbers pin a free body in the plane, six in
    space, a revolute joint contributes one freedom and a ball-and-socket three. Notice what
    the arithmetic buys: a planar arm with three hinged joints has DOF = 3 — three numbers
    describe it completely.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Grübler's formula</em> turns this into honest bookkeeping for linkages, where joints
    form loops that constrain each other: in the plane, DOF = 3 × (links − 1) − 2 × (one-
    freedom joints). Take the four-bar linkage — the mechanism inside your windshield wiper
    and a thousand engines: four links (including the ground), four revolute joints, one
    loop. DOF = 3 × (4 − 1) − 2 × 4 = 9 − 8 = 1. One input — spin the crank — and the whole
    mechanism's motion is determined. That single number is the design's certificate: the
    linkage will move predictably, never flop and never jam. Get the count wrong in either
    direction and no quality of motor rescues you: a mechanism with fewer degrees of freedom
    than actuators binds and fights itself; one with more flops uncontrolled.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the faulty line — the actuator audit" widget="spot-error" config={cfgGrublerSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Click the faulty line of the actuator audit and read the correction: it is the reverse —
    fewer degrees of freedom than actuators binds and fights itself, more and it flops
    uncontrolled. Notice the four-bar line that stays true: one input, spin the crank,
    determines the whole mechanism's motion.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more example cements the method, because it shows joints of different kinds sharing
    a formula. The slider–crank — the linkage that turns every car engine's piston into
    rotation — has four links (crank, connecting rod, piston, ground), three revolute joints
    and one prismatic (sliding) joint, all worth one freedom each. Grübler: DOF = 3 ×
    (4 − 1) − 2 × 4 = 1. Again exactly one: drive the crank and the piston must follow its
    prescribed dance, which is precisely what an engine requires — one input, one
    coordinated output. The formula's power is that it delivers this verdict from a parts
    list, before anything is built or simulated.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same counting explains a puzzling industry fact: industrial arms commonly have six
    joints, not five or seven. Six is the minimum that can place the gripper anywhere in
    space with any orientation (a free body's 6 DOF). Seven appears on the most capable
    arms — elephant trunks and human arms are redundancy of the same kind — giving the
    controller slack to reach around obstacles while holding the gripper pose fixed. In
    C-space terms: a 7-dimensional space, with a 6-dimensional "task surface" carved out
    inside it, and infinitely many points of the space projecting onto each point of the
    surface. That infinity is redundancy, and it is a feature.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="six joints or seven? file the reasons" widget="sort-buckets" config={cfgJointsSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> File the reasons under six or seven: six is the minimum to place the gripper anywhere in
    space with any orientation — the standard industrial arm — while seven buys slack to
    reach around obstacles with the gripper pose held fixed, a 6-dimensional task surface
    carved inside a 7-dimensional space. Watch the why: the infinity of configurations per
    pose is redundancy, a feature rather than a joint wasted.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a 7-DOF arm is a 6-DOF arm with one joint wasted. The redundancy is the point: the extra dimension gives the controller a one-parameter family of configurations for every gripper pose — self-motion it can spend dodging obstacles, avoiding singularities or easing joint limits while the hand stays fixed. Human arms work the same way, which is why you can reach your cup with your elbow tucked or flared: the task does not determine the arm.</p>

  <h2>Task and workspace</h2>
  <p>
    Three spaces matter, and keeping them distinct prevents half of all beginner confusion.
    The <em>configuration space</em> is the space of the robot's joint values. The
    <em>workspace</em> is the set of points the end-effector can physically reach — a
    property of the arm's geometry. The <em>task space</em> is whatever the job actually
    lives in: two dimensions for drawing on paper, six for spray-painting a car body, five
    or fewer for most assembly operations once alignment is handled by fixtures.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag between workspace and task space" widget="compare" config={cfgSpacesCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Drag between the two spaces and keep the third in view: the workspace is the points the end-
    effector can physically reach, at most six dimensions, while the task space is whatever
    the job lives in — two dimensions for drawing on paper, six for spray-painting a car
    body. Notice the why's punchline: planning happens in configuration space, the biggest
    one — the task never sees the difficulty.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two-link planar arm makes the workspace concrete. With links of one metre each, the
    gripper can reach any point within two metres of the shoulder — but only if the arm can
    fully extend, and it can also fold the gripper all the way back to the shoulder itself.
    The reachable set is therefore not a disk but an <em>annulus</em>: radii from 0 to 2 m.
    The geometry hides a classic computational wrinkle: most reachable points correspond to
    <em>two</em> configurations — "elbow up" and "elbow down" — so the mapping from task to
    configuration (inverse kinematics) is not one-to-one, and the planner must choose. The
    forward direction (configuration to position) is easy trigonometry; the inverse is where
    the mathematics gets interesting, and it is the inverse a robot actually needs.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="place the annulus's outer edge" widget="place" config={cfgAnnulusPlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Place the outer edge of the reachable set: two one-metre links fully extended reach 1 + 1 =
    2 m, so the marker lands on the 2 m label. Notice the why's wrinkle: the inner radius is
    0 — the arm folds the gripper back to the shoulder — so the reachable set is an annulus,
    and most points correspond to two configurations, elbow up or elbow down.
  </p>
  <p>
    The spaces also differ in the direction of difficulty. Workspaces are small — at most
    six dimensions, usually fewer — while configuration spaces grow with every joint, and
    planning happens in the bigger one. A task as humble as "wipe this tabletop" may demand
    navigating a 6-dimensional C-space to keep the wrist legal while the hand traces a flat
    2-dimensional path. The task never sees the difficulty; the configuration space carries
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real arms also carry <em>joint limits</em>, which fence the C-space even in empty
    rooms: a hinge that cannot pass 180 degrees turns its angle line into a segment. Add
    gravity and the picture sharpens again — every point in C-space implies a torque load
    on every joint, so two configurations reaching the same task point can differ wildly in
    energy cost and payload capacity. This is why the C-space picture, fences and terrain
    included, is the planner's true map rather than a mathematician's conceit: the point
    that wanders into the wrong region breaks a robot, and the point that lingers on steep
    terrain burns a motor.
  </p>

  <h2>Holonomic and nonholonomic constraints</h2>
  <p>
    A <em>holonomic</em> constraint is a restriction on configurations themselves — the
    classic is a door hinge: the door's angle is fixed to the wall's, full stop. Holonomic
    constraints shrink the C-space (the hinge's two links are really one DOF), and the
    planner can simply plan in the smaller space.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Nonholonomic</em> constraints are the subtle kind: restrictions on <em>velocity</em>
    that do not reduce the set of reachable positions. The canonical example is a car. Its
    configuration needs three numbers in the plane — position x, y and heading θ — so its
    C-space is 3-dimensional. But the driver controls only two things: steering angle and
    forward speed. Rolling without slipping forbids sideways motion: the car's velocity must
    point along its heading, always. There is no configuration the car cannot eventually
    reach — yet it cannot move sideways <em>one centimetre</em>, ever.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> nonholonomic constraints reduce the robot's degrees of
    freedom. They do not — the car's C-space stays 3-dimensional, and every point in it is
    reachable. What they constrain is the <em>directions of available motion at each point</em>,
    and therefore the shape of feasible paths. Parallel parking is the everyday proof: the
    gap is trivially reachable in C-space, but only through a curved, back-and-forth
    manoeuvre, because the straight path would require the forbidden sideways slide. The
    distinction matters enormously in planning: a holonomic robot can plan point-to-point
    and follow any path; a car-like robot must plan paths that respect its turning radius,
    which is a genuinely harder computational problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Obstacles move into the space too</h2>
  <p>
    The C-space's cleverest trick is what it does to collisions. Checking whether a shaped,
    articulated robot intersects a shaped world, joint by joint, is a geometry nightmare —
    and you would have to redo it for every instant of every candidate path. C-space
    replaces the nightmare with a map: compute, once, the set of <em>configurations</em> in
    which the robot would collide with anything, and mark that whole set as forbidden
    terrain. The living room's sofa becomes a curved region in joint-angle space; the
    question "can the arm reach the shelf?" becomes "can a path dodge that region?" — the
    same question a GPS answers, in stranger coordinates. Computing the forbidden region
    exactly is hard for high dimensions, which is one more reason planners end up sampling
    and testing points rather than mapping the terrain in full.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="order the collision trick" widget="order" config={cfgObstacleOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Sequence the collision trick and watch the nightmare become a map: compute once the set of
    configurations where the robot would collide, mark the whole set as forbidden terrain —
    the sofa becomes a curved region in joint-angle space — and the reach question becomes
    path-dodging. Notice the final step: exact computation is hard in high dimensions, so
    planners sample and test points instead.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> it is enough to plan the gripper's path around the obstacle and let the rest of the arm follow. The arm is not a point: every link sweeps its own volume, so the same sofa that occupies one modest shape in the room becomes a large curved region in joint space — and a path that looks safely around the obstacle in the workspace can pass straight through its C-space shadow with an elbow. That growth of obstacles under the mapping is why "steer the wrist, ignore the elbow" is the classic first-planner bug.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the room's sofa becomes a curved forbidden region in joint-angle space, and it grows under
    the mapping — which is why a path that clears the obstacle in the workspace can still
    clip it with an elbow.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A four-bar linkage has four links and four revolute joints. What does Grübler's formula give, and what does that single number certify about the design?</li>
    <li>The driver controls only two things — steering and speed — yet the car's C-space is 3-dimensional. Why is nothing missing, and what does parallel parking prove?</li>
    <li>Why can a gripper path that looks safely around a sofa in the workspace still collide with it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) DOF = 3 × (4 − 1) − 2 × 4 = 9 − 8 = 1: one input, spin the
    crank, determines the whole mechanism's motion — the certificate that it will move
    predictably, never flop and never jam. (2) A planar car configuration needs three numbers
    (x, y, heading); the nonholonomic rolling constraint restricts the <em>directions of
    motion</em> at each point, not the reachable set — every point is reachable, but only by
    paths that respect the turning radius, which is exactly what the back-and-forth parallel
    park demonstrates. (3) The robot is not a point: every link sweeps its own volume, so the
    sofa grows into a large curved region of joint-angle space, and a path that clears it in
    the workspace can pass straight through its C-space shadow with an elbow — the classic
    first-planner bug.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The configuration space replaces the whole robot with one point: "where is the robot?" becomes "where is the point?".</li>
    <li>DOF is the dimension of C-space, and Grübler's formula certifies a linkage's motion from a parts list alone.</li>
    <li>Industrial arms have six joints because a free body needs 6 DOF; the seventh buys redundancy — self-motion to spend on obstacle and singularity avoidance.</li>
    <li>Configuration space, workspace and task space are distinct; planning happens in the biggest one, and the task never sees the difficulty.</li>
    <li>Nonholonomic constraints restrict velocities, not positions: the car can reach anywhere, yet never slides sideways a centimetre.</li>
    <li>World obstacles become forbidden regions in C-space — and they grow under the mapping (the Minkowski sum).</li>
    <li>Grids die of dimensionality; sampling-based planners trade guaranteed optimality for probabilistic completeness.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    C-spaces have topology — joint angles wrap into circles and two-joint arms live on a torus
    — and planners that ignore the wrap-around plan across a seam that does not exist. The
    sampling-based planners, RRT and probabilistic roadmaps, are the field's answer to
    dimension, and the open frontier runs through planning under uncertainty and contact, and
    whole-body humanoid planning where dynamics, balance and foot-placement constraints all
    bind at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    C-spaces have topology, and it bites. A single revolute joint's angle lives on a circle:
    359 degrees is adjacent to 0, not to 720. A two-joint arm therefore lives not on a
    rectangle but on a torus — a doughnut, where "past the edge" wraps around. Planners that
    ignore the wrap-around plan across a seam that does not exist, producing spurious
    "collisions" at the 0/360 boundary. The torus is the friendliest case; closed-chain
    mechanisms have C-spaces whose very dimension can change from place to place, and whose
    geometry researchers still characterise case by case.
  </p>
  <p>
    Obstacles in the world grow when mapped into C-space — the technical device is the
    Minkowski sum: a round robot sweeping past a corner forbids a larger, corner-round
    region, every configuration where any part of the robot would touch. And dimension
    remains the enemy, quantifiably. Discretise each joint at a modest 63 values (0.1 radian
    steps over a typical range): a 2-DOF space is 3,969 cells — trivial. Six DOF is about
    6.25 × 10¹⁰ — dead on arrival. A humanoid's 30-plus DOF makes storing the grid, never
    mind searching it, physically absurd. This is precisely why sampling-based planners —
    the RRT family and probabilistic roadmaps — took over the field: they probe the space
    with random samples, connect near neighbours, and grow trees or roadmaps sparsely,
    never enumerating. They surrender guaranteed optimality and even guaranteed success,
    keeping only <em>probabilistic completeness</em> — given a solution exists, the
    probability of finding one approaches 1 as sampling continues. That trade — certainty
    about completeness for practicality about dimension — is the defining bargain of modern
    motion planning, and the open questions run from planning under uncertainty and contact
    (where C-space itself becomes probabilistic) to whole-body planning for humanoids, where
    dynamics, balance and nonholonomic foot-placement constraints all bind at once.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the grid's death" widget="estimate" config={cfgGridEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Estimate the grid's size before revealing: 63 values per joint on a 2-DOF space gives 63 ×
    63 = 3,969 cells — trivial. Notice the cliff in the why: six DOF is about 6.25 × 10¹⁰
    cells, dead on arrival, and a humanoid's 30-plus is physically absurd to store — hence
    the sampling-based planners, RRT and probabilistic roadmaps, which never enumerate.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lynch &amp; Park, <em>Modern Robotics</em>, Ch. 2 (official companion site); LaValle, <em>Planning Algorithms</em>. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
